│ Okay, let’s address this. Based on the       │
│ previous discussion and the understanding    │
│ that Django 2.2+ changed the behavior of     │
│ get_FIELD_display(), the issue stems from a  │
│ lack of explicit handling of the case where  │
│ the field doesn’t have a display value. In   │
│ Django 2.1, it would implicitly return the   │
│ field’s name. In 2.2+, it returns None,      │
│ which is then treated as an empty string by  │
│ __str__, leading to the "something" output.  │
│                                              │
│ The fix is to explicitly return the field’s  │
│ name when the display value is None. This    │
│ ensures consistent behavior across Django    │
│ versions.                                    │
│                                              │
│ ┏━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━┓ │
